<h1>3.4 Refresh-ahead</h1>
<p class="lead">With normal expiration, some unlucky user always pays for the reload. Refresh-ahead reloads popular entries in the background shortly before they expire, so users keep getting fast hits.</p>

<div class="presenter" data-video="3.4" hidden></div>
<div class="story" data-story="3.4"></div>

<div class="rw-head"><h2>&#127757; In the real world</h2></div>
<p class="rw-sub">How the biggest companies on the internet meet the idea from this lesson.</p>
<div class="rw-grid">
    <div class="rw-card" style="--rw:#e50914">
        <div class="rw-company"><span class="rw-logo">🎬</span>Netflix</div>
        <div class="rw-stat">Recommendations precomputed<small>ready before you open the app</small></div>
        <p class="rw-text">Netflix computes much of your personalized homepage ahead of time, offline and nearline, and stores the results in caches like EVCache. When you open the app, the rows are already waiting instead of being calculated while you wait.</p>
        <div class="rw-lesson"><b>Lesson:</b> Refreshing data before users need it removes the slow first request.</div>
        <div class="rw-source">Source: Netflix Tech Blog, &quot;System Architectures for Personalization and Recommendation&quot;, 2013</div>
    </div>
</div>



<details class="deep">
<summary>&#128214; Want to understand it deeply? Read the full explanation</summary>

<h2>The problem it solves</h2>
<p>With cache-aside and a 20-second TTL, the entry disappears at second 20 and the next user waits for the database. For a page that gets 100 requests per second, that is a visible spike in latency every 20 seconds, and a burst of database load if many requests miss at once.</p>

<h2>How it works</h2>
<p>Each entry has a TTL (20 s) and a <b>refresh threshold</b> (75% of the TTL, so 15 s). When a read finds an entry older than the threshold, it returns the cached value immediately <b>and</b> starts a background reload. When the reload finishes, the fresh value replaces the old one with a new 20-second TTL.</p>
<div class="flow">
    <div class="flow-step hit"><b>0 to 15 s</b><small>Normal hits</small></div>
    <div class="flow-arrow">&rarr;</div>
    <div class="flow-step"><b>15 to 20 s</b><small>Hit + background refresh starts</small></div>
    <div class="flow-arrow">&rarr;</div>
    <div class="flow-step hit"><b>Refresh done</b><small>New value, TTL restarts</small></div>
    <div class="flow-arrow">/</div>
    <div class="flow-step miss"><b>No read before 20 s</b><small>Expires normally, next read waits</small></div>
</div>
<p>A small dictionary of "refresh in progress" flags makes sure only <b>one</b> background refresh runs per key, even if many requests arrive in the refresh window.</p>
<div class="callout">
    <strong>Compared with stale-while-revalidate</strong>
    This is the same idea as the HTTP <code>stale-while-revalidate</code> from lesson 2.1, but inside your application. Refresh-ahead starts before expiry, so the value is never stale. Stale-while-revalidate serves a value that has already expired.
</div>

</details>

<h2><span class="try-it">Try it</span>Live demo</h2>
<p>Both cards read the same product with the same 20-second TTL. Only the right card refreshes ahead.</p>
<div class="card">
    <div class="toolbar">
        <label class="field">Product
            <select id="productId"></select>
        </label>
        <label class="check"><input type="checkbox" id="auto" /> Read both every 3 seconds automatically</label>
    </div>
</div>
<div class="grid grid-2">
    @foreach (var (strategy, title) in new[] { ("cache-aside", "Plain expiration (cache-aside)"), ("refresh-ahead", "Refresh-ahead (refresh after 15 s)") })
    {
        <div class="card" data-strategy="@strategy">
            <b>@title</b>
            <div class="countdown" data-role="age">-</div>
            <div class="muted">age of the cached copy</div>
            <div class="toolbar">
                <button class="btn primary" data-role="get">Read</button>
                <button class="btn danger" data-role="remove">Remove</button>
            </div>
            <div class="result-box" data-role="result">-</div>
            <div class="muted" data-role="slow">Slow reads (user waited for the database): 0</div>
        </div>
    }
</div>
<div class="card">
    <b>Event log</b>
    <div class="events">
        <table>
            <thead><tr><th>Time</th><th>Event</th><th>Key</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody id="events"></tbody>
        </table>
    </div>
</div>

<h2>Experiments: do these in order</h2>
<ol class="steps">
    <li><b>Simulate steady traffic.</b> Tick <em>Read both every 3 seconds automatically</em> and watch for a minute.</li>
    <li><b>Plain expiration.</b> On the left, the age climbs to 20 s, then one read is slow (800 ms) and the age restarts. The slow-read counter keeps growing.</li>
    <li><b>Refresh-ahead.</b> On the right, after 15 s a read logs "started a background refresh", shortly followed by <span class="badge refreshed">Refreshed</span>, and the age restarts without any slow read. Only the very first read was slow.</li>
    <li><b>Unpopular data.</b> Untick the box and wait 25 s. Refresh-ahead only refreshes on reads, so an entry nobody reads expires normally. That is a feature: it does not waste work on cold data.</li>
</ol>

<details class="deep">
<summary>&#128214; More details (optional)</summary>

<h2>Choosing a strategy</h2>
<div class="card">
    <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Situation</th><th>Good choice</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
            <tr><td>General web app, simple needs</td><td>Cache-aside + write-around (delete on write)</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Many services read the same data, you want one loading rule</td><td>Read-through (HybridCache, Level 4)</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Users read their data right after saving it</td><td>Write-through</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Huge write volume, small losses acceptable (views, likes, scores)</td><td>Write-behind with a durable queue</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Hot data that is expensive to load, latency spikes hurt</td><td>Refresh-ahead</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Data must be 100% current (stock during checkout, balances)</td><td>Do not cache, or cache with very short TTL and validation</td></tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>
</div>

</details>

<h2>What you should take away</h2>
<div class="callout tip">
    <strong>Key points</strong>
    Refresh-ahead keeps hot entries warm by reloading them in the background before they expire, so readers almost never wait. It needs a refresh threshold, a guard so only one refresh runs per key, and it should only refresh entries that are actually being read.
</div>
<div class="callout danger">
    <strong>Common mistakes</strong>
    Refreshing every key on a timer, including ones nobody reads, which multiplies database load. Starting one refresh per request in the window instead of one per key. Swallowing refresh errors silently so the cache quietly expires.
</div>

<div class="callout">
    <strong>Level 3 complete</strong>
    You can now name and compare all six classic strategies and pick a combination for a given situation. Level 4 moves the cache out of a single server: distributed caching with Redis.
</div>

